# 🚀 EKS Static Application Deployment with Ingress (ALB)

## 📌 Overview

This document explains how to:

* Create an EKS cluster using `eksctl`
* Deploy a static NGINX application
* Configure Ingress using AWS Load Balancer Controller
* Troubleshoot common errors

---

# 🧰 Prerequisites

* AWS CLI configured (`aws configure`)
* kubectl installed
* eksctl installed
* Helm installed
* IAM permissions for EKS

---

# 🏗️ Step 1: Create EKS Cluster

```
eksctl create cluster \
  --name test-cluster \
  --region ap-south-1 \
  --nodegroup-name test-nodes \
  --node-type t3.medium \
  --nodes 2
```

---

# 🔍 Step 2: Verify Cluster

```bash
kubectl get nodes
```

---

# 🌐 Step 3: Deploy NGINX Application

```bash
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: nginx-deployment
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: nginx
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: nginx
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: nginx
        image: nginx
        resources:
          requests:
            cpu: "100m"
            memory: "128Mi"
          limits:
            cpu: "200m"
            memory: "256Mi" 
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80
```

```
 kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml
```

---

# 🔗 Step 4: Create Service

```yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: nginx-service
spec:
  type: ClusterIP
  selector:
    app: nginx
  ports:
    - port: 80
      targetPort: 80
```

```bash
kubectl apply -f service.yaml
```

---

# ⚙️ Step 5: Setup ALB Ingress Controller

## 5.1 Enable OIDC

```bash
eksctl utils associate-iam-oidc-provider \
  --region ap-south-1 \
  --cluster test-cluster \
  --approve
```

---

## 5.2 Create IAM Policy

```bash
aws iam create-policy \
  --policy-name AWSLoadBalancerControllerIAMPolicy \
  --policy-document file://iam_policy.json
```

---

## 5.3 Create IAM Service Account (IRSA)

```bash
eksctl create iamserviceaccount \
  --cluster=test-cluster \
  --namespace=kube-system \
  --name=aws-load-balancer-controller \
  --attach-policy-arn=arn:aws:iam::<ACCOUNT_ID>:policy/AWSLoadBalancerControllerIAMPolicy \
  --approve
```

---

## 5.4 Install Controller using Helm

```bash
helm repo add eks https://aws.github.io/eks-charts
helm repo update

helm install aws-load-balancer-controller eks/aws-load-balancer-controller \
  -n kube-system \
  --set clusterName=test-cluster \
  --set serviceAccount.create=false \
  --set serviceAccount.name=aws-load-balancer-controller
```

---

# 🌍 Step 6: Create Ingress

```yaml
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  name: nginx-ingress
  annotations:
    alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/scheme: internet-facing
    alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/target-type: ip
spec:
  ingressClassName: alb
  rules:
    - http:
        paths:
          - path: /
            pathType: Prefix
            backend:
              service:
                name: nginx-service
                port:
                  number: 80
```

```bash
kubectl apply -f ingress.yaml
```

---

# 🔍 Step 7: Get Application URL

```bash
kubectl get ingress
```

Wait for:

```
ADDRESS: xxxxx.elb.amazonaws.com
```

---

# 🐞 Common Errors & Fixes

---

## ❌ Error 1: Helm not found

```
zsh: command not found: helm
```

✅ Fix:

```bash
brew install helm
```

---

## ❌ Error 2: IAM Policy Already Exists

```
EntityAlreadyExists
```

✅ Fix:

* Reuse existing policy ARN instead of creating new

---

## ❌ Error 3: Wrong IAM ARN

```
Unauthorized
```

✅ Fix:

* Use correct format:

```
arn:aws:iam::<ACCOUNT_ID>:policy/AWSLoadBalancerControllerIAMPolicy
```

---

## ❌ Error 4: ServiceAccount already exists

```
excluded, use override
```

✅ Fix:

```bash
--override-existing-serviceaccounts
```

---

## ❌ Error 5: CrashLoopBackOff (ALB Controller)

Cause:

* IAM not attached properly

✅ Fix:

* Delete and recreate service account
* Restart deployment

---

## ❌ Error 6: Ingress ADDRESS empty

Cause:

* Missing ingress class or annotations

✅ Fix:

```yaml
ingressClassName: alb
```

---

## ❌ Error 7: Authentication error (kubectl)

```
the server has asked for credentials
```

✅ Fix:

```bash
aws eks update-kubeconfig --region ap-south-1 --name test-cluster
```

---

## ❌ Error 8: DNS not resolving

```
DNS_PROBE_POSSIBLE
```

Cause:

* ALB still provisioning

✅ Fix:

* Wait 2–5 minutes

---

# 🧠 Key Learnings

* `eksctl` uses CloudFormation internally
* Ingress requires ALB Controller
* IAM (IRSA) is critical for AWS integration
* One ALB can serve multiple apps (cost optimization)

---

# 💸 Cleanup

```bash
eksctl delete cluster --name test-cluster --region ap-south-1
```

---

# 🎯 Conclusion

You successfully:

* Created EKS cluster
* Deployed application
* Configured Ingress with ALB
* Debugged real-world issues

